%% ============================================================
%% F1: All dt>1 degrade
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F1: All Variable-Rate Configurations Degrade}
\label{sec:f1}

Every configuration that introduces $\dt > 1$ imagination degrades relative to the $\dt{=}1$ baseline, regardless of mechanism (Figure~\ref{fig:learning_curves}). The baseline (exp\_001) reaches 915.7 at 105K steps and peaks at 970.4 at 505K. In contrast, fixed $\dt{=}2$ (exp\_006) peaks at 485.3 at 35K before collapsing to 132.9 by 65K---a $-73\%$ decline from its own peak (14\% of baseline). Fixed $\dt{=}4$ (exp\_007) peaks at only 176.9 at 25K and never exceeds 152.5 thereafter. The learned-gate configuration (exp\_005) peaks at 541.2 at 55K but collapses to 98.8 by 145K. This uniform degradation motivates the factorial analysis that follows.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_learning_curves.pdf}
\caption{Learning curves for all configurations. The $\dt{=}1$ baseline (black) reaches 915.7 at 105K; all variable-rate configurations degrade, with fixed $\dt{=}4$ and the learned gate collapsing most severely.}
\label{fig:learning_curves}
\end{figure}

%% ============================================================
%% F2: Trained dt_embedding → nve > 1.5x
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F2: Trained Temporal Embedding Triggers Value Overestimation}
\label{sec:f2}

The factorial design isolates trained $\dt$-embedding as the factor that reliably triggers pathological value overestimation. The key causal control compares exp\_008d (trained $\dt$-embedding, subsampling ON, $H{=}8$) against exp\_008f (identical configuration but \emph{no} trained $\dt$-embedding). As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:nve_trajectory}:

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{008d} (trained): $\nve = 1.78\times$ at 25K $\rightarrow$ $4.12\times$ at 55K $\rightarrow$ collapse (eval 167$\rightarrow$82.8).
\item \textbf{008f} (no dt\_emb): $\nve = 1.02\times$ at 25K $\rightarrow$ $0.30\times$ at 105K $\rightarrow$ no collapse (eval 503.0 at 105K).
\end{itemize}

In the 35K+ window (\S\ref{sec:metrics}), the $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ threshold correctly classifies 7/8 seeds (AUROC${=}0.933$, Clopper--Pearson 95\% CI $[0.47, 1.0]$): 3 CIVO-positive (exp\_006, 008d, 009b) and 4 CIVO-negative, with one non-CIVO false positive (008e\_v3 s2, $3.78\times$; \S\ref{sec:f6}).  The gap between the lowest true positive ($1.62\times$) and the highest true negative ($1.00\times$) is $0.62$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig2_nve_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{Normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\nve$) trajectories. The dashed line marks the $1.5\times$ threshold. All trained-embedding configurations with $\dt > 1$ imagination (red/orange) exceed this threshold; the mitigation configuration (008e\_v3 s1, dash-dot) with $\dt{=}1$-only imagination remains below it.}
\label{fig:nve_trajectory}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Three post-CIVO outcomes.}
Running the 008d configuration with two independent seeds (s0, s2) and an extended replication of seed 1 (009e, continuing the same training run) reveals that trained $\dt$-embedding triggers CIVO---yet the downstream consequence is stochastic:

%% ============================================================
%% TABLE 3: Three post-CIVO outcomes
%% ============================================================

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Post-CIVO outcomes for the 008d configuration. Peak/Final NVE measured in the 35K+ window. Seeds 0 and 2 are independent runs; seed 1 (009e) is an extended replication.}
\label{tab:post_civo}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcccl}
\toprule
\textbf{Seed} & \textbf{Peak} & \textbf{Final} & \textbf{Eval} & \textbf{Outcome} \\
\midrule
s0 (008d) & 4.12$\times$ & 4.12$\times$ & 82.8 & Full collapse \\
s2 (009b) & 1.62$\times$ & 0.38$\times$ & 267.9 & Transient CIVO \\
s1 (009e) & 4.12$\times$ & 1.57$\times$ & 131.1 & Oscillatory \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\noindent\textbf{Seed 0 (008d):} Full collapse (Pathway A). Value explosion ($+130\%$), evaluation declines from 167 to 82.8, entropy decreases---the classic CIVO feedback loop.

\noindent\textbf{Seed 2 (009b):} Transient CIVO. The $\nve$ reaches $1.62\times$ at 45K (35K+ window), exceeding the $1.5\times$ threshold, but the value function self-corrects to $0.38\times$ by 105K, and evaluation recovers to 267.9. This demonstrates the threshold's sensitivity to early CIVO signals even in cases that spontaneously recover.

\noindent\textbf{Seed 1 (009e):} Oscillatory degradation. Evaluation oscillates between 82.7 and 250 before settling at 131.1; the value function rises then reverses (peak $\nve = 4.12\times$ at 55K, final $1.57\times$).

All runs exceed $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ in the 35K+ window, confirming deterministic CIVO triggering, but downstream outcomes are seed-dependent (1/2 persistent collapse rate among independent seeds).

%% ============================================================
%% F3: Untrained dt_embedding prevents drift
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F3: Untrained Temporal Embedding Prevents Drift}
\label{sec:f3}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig3_factorial_grid.pdf}
\caption{Factorial experiment summary. Each cell shows final evaluation return and $\nve$ status. Trained $\dt$-embedding is the sole factor that separates collapsed (red) from healthy (green) configurations.}
\label{fig:factorial_grid}
\end{figure}

When the $\dt$-embedding is present but \emph{untrained} (fixed random projection), value dynamics remain healthy: exp\_008a reaches 777.7 ($\nve = 0.59\times$) and exp\_008c reaches 240.9 ($\nve = 0.58\times$), with no pathological overestimation.  This identifies \emph{training} of the temporal embedding---not its architectural presence---as the causal factor: the untrained embedding provides a temporal signal without co-adapting with the reward head.

%% ============================================================
%% F4: dt=2 > dt=4 monotonic degradation
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F4: Degradation Scales Monotonically with \texorpdfstring{$\dt$}{dt}}
\label{sec:f4}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig5_dt_amplification.pdf}
\caption{Peak evaluation return as a function of imagination $\dt$. Performance degrades monotonically: $\dt{=}1$ (915.7) $>$ $\dt{=}2$ (485.3, $-47\%$) $>$ $\dt{=}4$ (176.9, $-81\%$).}
\label{fig:dt_amplification}
\end{figure}

The magnitude of degradation scales monotonically with the imagination step size (Figure~\ref{fig:dt_amplification}). From the $\dt{=}1$ baseline (915.7 at 105K), fixed $\dt{=}2$ (exp\_006) achieves a peak of only 485.3 ($-47\%$), and fixed $\dt{=}4$ (exp\_007) peaks at 176.9 ($-81\%$). This monotonic relationship is consistent with compounding model error: larger temporal gaps amplify the RSSM's prediction inaccuracy at each imagination step, and these errors accumulate across the rollout horizon.

%% ============================================================
%% F5: Horizon and subsample secondary
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F5: Imagination Horizon and Subsampling Are Secondary Factors}
\label{sec:f5}

Neither horizon length nor temporal subsampling triggers collapse without a trained $\dt$-embedding: both untrained configurations maintain $\nve < 0.6\times$ (\S\ref{sec:f3}), while the trained counterpart (008d) collapses with $\nve = 4.12\times$.  The no-embedding configurations show cross-seed variance (008f: 503.0 vs.\ 009c: 148.3) but neither exhibits $\nve > 1.5\times$---confirming that low evaluation alone does not indicate CIVO.

%% ============================================================
%% F6: dt=1 imagination does not prevent collapse
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F6: Restricting Imagination to \texorpdfstring{$\dt{=}1$}{dt=1} Does Not Prevent Collapse}
\label{sec:f6}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig6_multiple_pathways.pdf}
\caption{Contrasting collapse dynamics. \textbf{Left:} 008d (classic CIVO)---value explosion precedes evaluation decline, entropy decreases. \textbf{Right:} 008e\_v3 seed 2 (non-CIVO)---evaluation crashes while value remains stable, entropy \emph{increases}. Baseline seed 2 (009a, dashed) remains stable at 836.5, ruling out seed fragility.}
\label{fig:multiple_pathways}
\end{figure}

The mitigation experiment exp\_008e\_v3 restricts imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained $\dt$-embedding for observation conditioning. If CIVO were the sole vulnerability pathway, this restriction should prevent collapse. Results are mixed:

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Seed 1} (008e\_v3\_s1): Healthy. Eval 869.9 at 105K ($95\%$ of baseline), $\nve = 0.80\times$.
\item \textbf{Seed 2} (008e\_v3\_s2): Collapsed. Eval drops from 715 to 137 at 105K, $\nve = 3.78\times$.
\end{itemize}

The baseline with seed 2 (exp\_009a) is stable at 836.5 at 105K, ruling out inherent seed fragility as the explanation.

\paragraph{Distinct collapse mechanisms.}
The collapse dynamics of 008e\_v3\_s2 differ qualitatively from the classic CIVO observed in 008d (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, Figure~\ref{fig:multiple_pathways}):

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{008d (CIVO, Pathway A):} value explosion ($+130\%$) $\rightarrow$ evaluation decline $\rightarrow$ entropy decreases.
\item \textbf{008e\_v3\_s2 (non-CIVO, Pathway B):} evaluation crashes ($-81\%$) $\rightarrow$ value remains stable $\rightarrow$ entropy \emph{increases} ($-5.21 \rightarrow -3.83$).
\end{itemize}

In Pathway A, the critic drives collapse through systematic overestimation; in Pathway B, the actor destabilizes independently while the critic remains initially well-calibrated. Restricting imagination to $\dt{=}1$ prevents the CIVO mechanism---no early $\nve$ spike, no reward amplification---but seed-dependent late-stage policy instability (Pathway B) can still occur.

This case also produces the sole false positive in the $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ classification: 008e\_v3\_s2's late evaluation decline inflates $\nve$ to $3.78\times$ through a non-CIVO mechanism (\S\ref{sec:pathways}).

