\begin{abstract}
Temporal abstraction via variable-rate imagination is a natural extension of world-model reinforcement learning, yet we find that every variable-rate configuration of DreamerV3 degrades performance---despite the success of identical $\Delta t$-conditioning in MPC-based methods. Through a factorial diagnostic on Walker Walk, we identify trained temporal embedding as the causal factor and characterize the resulting failure mode: \emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO). The trained embedding enables the RSSM reward head to develop a $2.78\times$ prediction gap between $\Delta t{=}1$ and $\Delta t{=}2$, which the critic internalizes as optimistic values. A normalized value-to-eval threshold of $1.5\times$ (35K+ training window) achieves 7/8 classification ($n{=}8$, Walker Walk), with the single false positive arising from a mechanistically distinct non-CIVO collapse. The same CIVO trigger produces stochastic downstream outcomes across seeds---full collapse, transient CIVO with self-correction, and oscillatory degradation---from identical configurations. Critically, restricting imagination to $\Delta t{=}1$ does not prevent collapse: one of two seeds fails via a distinct non-CIVO pathway with increasing entropy, revealing that trained temporal conditioning creates vulnerability through multiple mechanisms. Our analysis identifies the feedback architecture---imagination-based actor-critic bootstrapping versus MPC re-planning---as the structural factor that transforms manageable model imprecision into pathological value dynamics.
\end{abstract}

