\section{Analysis \& Discussion}
\label{sec:analysis}

The preceding results establish that trained $\dt$-embedding is the causal factor behind pathological value dynamics in variable-rate DreamerV3. In this section, we synthesize these findings into a mechanistic account of conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO), explain why the same CIVO trigger produces divergent outcomes across seeds, contrast the imagination-based setting with MPC-based temporal conditioning, and discuss the fundamental tension inherent in the $\dt$-embedding design.

\subsection{The CIVO Mechanism}
\label{sec:civo_mechanism}

The RSSM fidelity analysis (Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}) reveals the mechanistic core of CIVO.  The trained $\dt$-embedding enables the reward head to learn temporally differentiated predictions, but these predictions are asymmetrically accurate: reward MAE at $\dt{=}1$ is $0.0568$, while at $\dt{=}2$ it rises to $0.1579$---a $2.78\times$ gap.  This gap is not an artifact of prediction difficulty alone; the no-embedding configuration (008f) exhibits a $1.01\times$ gap, and the baseline shows $1.56\times$.  The trained embedding actively amplifies the fidelity discrepancy by allowing the reward head to co-adapt with the temporal signal.

This asymmetric fidelity initiates a feedback loop with four stages: (1) the trained $\dt$-embedding causes RSSM predictions at $\dt > 1$ to carry systematically biased reward estimates; (2) the critic, trained entirely on imagined trajectories, internalizes these biased rewards as optimistic value estimates; (3) the actor exploits the overestimated regions of the value landscape, shifting its policy toward states that appear high-value only under the biased model; and (4) this policy shift further skews the distribution of imagined states, reinforcing the bias.  The diagnostic signature is a sustained $\nve$ above $1.5\times$, followed by evaluation collapse.

A random-policy control experiment ($N{=}100$ rollouts, 95\% bootstrap CI) decomposes CIVO into two necessary stages.  \textbf{Stage~1 (dynamics bias):} even under a random policy, $\dt{=}2$ imagination produces $1.68\times$ higher mean reward than $\dt{=}1$ in the trained-embedding model (008d)---the RSSM prior already generates reward-favorable trajectories at $\dt > 1$, independent of actor behavior.  \textbf{Stage~2 (actor exploitation):} the trained actor learns to exploit these biased regions, amplifying the ratio to $2.90\times$.  In the no-embedding control (008f), neither stage is present: the random-policy ratio is $1.11\times$ and the trained-actor ratio is $0.98\times$.  The decomposition attributes $\approx 36\%$ of the total effect to dynamics bias and $\approx 64\%$ to actor exploitation, confirming that both stages are necessary for CIVO to manifest.

This mechanism is analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional}, but differs in origin: delusional bias arises from policy-class constraints, while CIVO is model-induced---the reward head's co-adaptation with the temporal embedding produces systematically upward-biased predictions at $\dt > 1$.  The consequence (policy exploitation of overestimated regions) is similar, but the causal pathway is distinct.

\subsection{Three Post-CIVO Outcomes: Why Seeds Diverge}
\label{sec:seed_divergence}

The three post-CIVO outcomes (Table~\ref{tab:post_civo})---full collapse, transient CIVO, and oscillatory degradation---all cross the $\nve > 1.5\times$ threshold, yet the downstream damage is stochastic.  We hypothesize that the divergence relates to the critic's representational capacity at the time of CIVO onset.  \citet{lyle2022capacity} demonstrate that deep RL networks suffer progressive capacity loss, in which early-learned features become increasingly difficult to overwrite.  If the critic's capacity is already partially consumed when CIVO injects overestimation, the network cannot recalibrate (seed~0); if sufficient capacity remains, the critic can absorb transient overestimation and recover (seed~2).  The oscillatory pattern (seed~1, 009e) represents an intermediate regime: the value function rises then reverses while evaluation oscillates, and entropy remains elevated---suggesting the actor has not fully committed to the overestimated policy but cannot escape it.  This partial capacity loss allows the critic to respond to new data without fully correcting its biases.

\subsection{Why MPC Is Immune}
\label{sec:mpc_immune}

\citet{nhu2025tawm} show that $\dt$-conditioning benefits TD-MPC2, raising the question of why it fails in DreamerV3.  The critical difference is that MPC re-plans from real states at every step---imagination errors do not accumulate into the training signal because there is no critic bootstrapping from imagined trajectories.  In DreamerV3, the actor-critic trains entirely within imagination, and any systematic reward-prediction bias at $\dt > 1$ is amplified through the closed actor-critic loop.  In terms of the two-stage decomposition (\S\ref{sec:civo_mechanism}), MPC is immune because Stage~2 cannot occur: without imagination-based bootstrapping, the actor cannot exploit the dynamics bias.  This aligns with \citet{frauenknecht2025rollouts}, who find that rollout quality dominates model-based RL performance, and with \citet{tallec2019making}'s analysis of how temporal resolution mismatches destabilize value learning.

\subsection{The \texorpdfstring{$\dt$}{dt}-Embedding Dilemma}
\label{sec:embedding_dilemma}

The factorial results reveal a fundamental tension: removing the $\dt$-embedding (008f) eliminates CIVO but at a $13\times$ cost to reward prediction quality ($0.7469$ vs.\ $0.0568$ MAE)---the world model cannot distinguish temporal scales.  Restricting imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding (008e\_v3) prevents CIVO in 1 of 2 seeds but the other collapses via Pathway~B (\S\ref{sec:f6}).  Neither approach alone provides a complete solution.

\subsection{Multiple Vulnerability Pathways}
\label{sec:pathways}

The two collapse mechanisms identified in \S\ref{sec:f6}---CIVO (Pathway~A, value-driven, entropy decreasing) and non-CIVO instability (Pathway~B, eval-driven, entropy increasing)---have distinct diagnostic signatures but a shared root cause: the trained $\dt$-embedding's learned features affect both reward prediction across temporal scales (Pathway~A) and the representation space in which the actor operates (Pathway~B).  This explains why $\nve$ is sensitive but not perfectly specific: Pathway~B's late evaluation decline inflates $\nve$ through the same arithmetic as CIVO, yielding the sole false positive ($3.78\times$ for 008e\_v3 s2).  Distinguishing the two requires examining the temporal profile and entropy direction of collapse.  The existence of multiple pathways suggests that mitigation strategies focused exclusively on the imagination channel---restricting $\dt$, truncating rollouts, or smoothing rewards \citep{lee2024dreamsmooth}---may be insufficient.

\subsection{Implications for Temporal Abstraction in World Models}
\label{sec:implications}

CIVO identifies a structural incompatibility between flat temporal conditioning and imagination-based actor-critic training: the RSSM cannot maintain prediction fidelity across temporal resolutions when the embedding co-adapts with the reward head.  Hierarchical approaches---THICK \citep{gumbsch2024thick}, Hieros \citep{mattes2024hieros}, LS-Imagine \citep{li2025lsimagine}---may avoid this by maintaining separate dynamics per temporal scale.  Whether CIVO transfers to transformer-based world models (DreamerV4; \citealt{hafner2025dreamerv4}) depends on whether attention weights co-adapt with temporal embeddings analogously.  Regularization of the $\dt$-embedding (\eg, gradient stopping or spectral normalization) could preserve the temporal signal while preventing co-adaptation; we leave empirical evaluation to future work.

